""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/12394"} "Pedro Mayorga"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ARG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ARG.png"}] "ARG"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/13022"} "Kohei Yusa"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Course Designer"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/12224"} "Shunzo Kido"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The competition consisted of two rounds, with total scores for both rounds to count towards final placements. The course was 780 metres, and was designed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/12224"} "Shunzo Kido"] ", a 1928-32 Olympian. There were 14 obstacles, with 17 jumps. The major obstacles came near the end, with the next-to-last obstacle a water jump of 5.00 metres, then, after a left turn, an oxer measuring 1.45 x 1.50 x 1.85 metres. It had rained for several days, which had made the ground very deep and soft, but it was fortunately not slippery."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The last three European Championships had been won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/12361"} "Graziano Mancinelli"] " (1963), " [:a {:href "/athletes/11591"} "David B. Barker"] " (1962), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/11677"} "David Broome"] " (1961). There had been no World Championships since the 1960 Olympics. The leaders after the first round were Britain " [:a {:href "/athletes/12630"} "Peter Robeson"] ", Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/11947"} "John Fahey"] ", and Portugal’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11899"} "Joaquim Silva"] ", all of whom had 8.00 faults. The 1952 gold medalist, " [:a {:href "/athletes/12194"} "Pierre Jonquères d’Oriola"] ", was alone in fourth place with 9.00 faults. In the second round, Jonquères d’Oriola had a clean ride, giving him a total of 9.00 faults and allowing him to win a second gold medal in the event, 12 years after his first. German " [:a {:href "/athletes/12714"} "Hermann Schridde"] " had the next best ride of the second round, with only 1.25 faults, giving him the silver medal with 13.75 faults. Robeson and Fahey tied at 16.00 faults and rode-off, with Robeson riding cleanly for the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "Horse"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Faults"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/12194"} "Pierre Jonquères d'Oriola"]] [:td {} [:i {} [:a {:href "/horses/1521"} "Lutteur"]]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "9.00"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/12714"} "Hermann Schridde"]] [:td {} [:i {} [:a {:href "/horses/690"} "Dozent"]]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "13.75"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/12630"} "Peter Robeson"]] [:td {} [:i {} [:a {:href "/horses/892"} "Firecrest"]]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [